        <body>
            <div xml:id="d735">

                <pb facs="0716" n="600" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg600"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.773/25.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Consul Williamson to
                        the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Dalny</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-10-09">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 9, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith this
                    office’s English translation of railway notice No. 46<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d735-f1"/> which contains what is known as the local reduced rates
                    on goods from the three seaports Dairen, Antung and Newchwang to the interior.
                    The rates from Dairen and Antung to Mukden and Tiehling have always been
                    identical; there is no special rate in this category on goods from Newchwang to
                    these places. In the new special rates made in this order No. 46, these
                    conditions are practically maintained, and the reduction amounts to 15 per cent
                    except that to Tiehling from Dairen and Antung, where it is 17 per cent. Thus
                    the old rate discrimination against Newchwang is continued and the extra
                    distances to Mukden of 137 miles for Dairen and 66.4 miles for Antung are still
                    wiped out by the rates.</p>
                <p>This step has been taken by the railway and sanctioned by the national Government
                    on account, chiefly, of the exertions of business men at Dairen who demanded
                    some such arrangement in order to put them on more even terms with those
                    shippers using the Antung route to the interior.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">A. A. Williamson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
